---
layout: app-layout.njk
title: Help – Climbing Logbook
bundle: help
---
<!-- #876 -- chained layout (this file's own front matter sets `layout:
     app-layout.njk` above), not a standalone <html>/<head>/<body> shell
     like the very first pass here -- Raven's call, 2026-09-20: /help
     should use the exact same header row every other app page does
     (full-width, brand left-aligned, <climbing-page-header> + burger
     menu for navigating back out), not a bespoke narrower shell of its
     own. app-layout.njk's own trailing bundle-script tag is now
     conditional specifically so this chain works -- see that file's
     own comment. -->

<!-- #876 -- Pagefind's Component UI assets + theme-variable overrides,
     placed here (inside <body>, at the top of this page's own content)
     rather than in app-layout.njk's <head> -- a chained layout's content
     only ever lands inside {{ content }}, past the point <head> is
     already closed. A stylesheet/script tag works identically wherever
     it sits in the document; nothing here needs to block first paint.
     See the file's own git history (first pass, before this restructure)
     for why the bundle-path config below is explicit rather than relying
     on the Component UI's documented (but not, in practice, working in
     this setup) auto-detection from the script's own src. -->
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/help/pagefind/pagefind-component-ui.css">
<script src="/help/pagefind/pagefind-component-ui.js" type="module"></script>
<style>
  :root {
    --pf-text: var(--color-text);
    --pf-text-secondary: var(--color-text-muted);
    --pf-text-muted: var(--color-text-muted);
    --pf-background: var(--color-surface);
    --pf-border: var(--color-border);
    --pf-border-focus: var(--color-accent);
    --pf-outline-focus: var(--color-accent);
    --pf-mark: var(--color-accent);
    --pf-hover: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 8%, transparent);
    --pf-skeleton: var(--color-border);
    --pf-skeleton-shine: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-border) 60%, transparent);
    --pf-font: var(--font-sans);
  }
</style>

<div class="max-w-[960px] mx-auto">
  <!-- admin-hidden: help is reachable logged-out same as the public
       profile page (views/profile/index.njk's own admin-hidden
       precedent) -- no session-gated content exists here to show a
       logged-in owner either, so there's nothing the burger menu's
       normal admin rows would ever have to display. The burger menu's
       own real job here is simply "a way back out of /help" -- no
       discipline picker (help has no discipline concept at all). -->
  <climbing-page-header admin-hidden></climbing-page-header>

  <!-- #876 -- two-column help view starts below the header row, per
       Raven's own instruction (2026-09-20) -- not part of the header
       row itself the way climbing-tab-bar sits on other pages.
       max-[600px]:flex-col: same breakpoint climbing-grade-pyramid.js/
       the Performance Insights hub's own tile grid already collapse at
       for narrow viewports -- the sidebar stacks above the article
       full-width on mobile rather than being squeezed beside it. -->
  <div class="flex gap-8 mt-6 max-[600px]:flex-col" id="help-layout">
    <!-- #876 -- built from eleventyNavigation's own collection API, not
         a hand-rolled tree: every views/help/** page contributes itself
         via its own front matter (`eleventyNavigation: { key, parent,
         order }`), so adding a new help page never means editing this
         file. flex-[0_0_14rem] (not a fraction) on wide viewports -- a
         fixed sidebar width reads better than one that stretches with
         viewport width the way the 2-column report grids elsewhere in
         this app deliberately do (those grids hold peer content of
         equal weight; a nav rail and its article are not peers). Full
         width once stacked (max-[600px]:flex-[0_0_auto]). -->
    <nav class="flex-[0_0_14rem] max-[600px]:flex-[0_0_auto] min-w-0" aria-label="Help topics">
      <!-- pagefind-config: see this file's own top-of-content comment. -->
      <pagefind-config bundle-path="/help/pagefind/"></pagefind-config>
      <!-- pagefind-searchbox is a self-registering custom element (the
           module script above defines it) -- no init call needed,
           unlike the older Default UI's `new PagefindUI(...)`.
           hide-shortcut + an unreachable shortcut combo: Raven's call
           (2026-09-20) to drop the Ctrl+K/Cmd+K affordance entirely, not
           just its visual hint -- confirmed via Pagefind's own docs that
           hide-shortcut only hides the hint pill, it doesn't stop the
           listener itself; there's no documented "disable the binding"
           attribute, so an effectively-unreachable combo is the
           pragmatic equivalent of off. mb-8, not mb-5 -- confirmed live
           that 5 read as touching the list below it once the component's
           own internal padding is accounted for. -->
      <pagefind-searchbox class="block mb-8" hide-shortcut shortcut="ctrl+alt+shift+meta+f24"></pagefind-searchbox>
      <ul class="m-0 p-0 list-none flex flex-col gap-1">
        {%- for entry in collections.all | eleventyNavigation %}
        <li>
          <a
            class="block px-3 py-[.4rem] rounded-app text-[.85rem] {% if entry.url == page.url %}bg-surface text-accent font-semibold{% else %}text-muted hover:text-foreground{% endif %}"
            href="{{ entry.url }}"
            {% if entry.url == page.url %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}
          >{{ entry.title }}</a>
        </li>
        {%- endfor %}
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <!-- data-pagefind-body: without it, Pagefind indexes every <body>
         element on the page (confirmed live -- its own build output
         warns "Did not find a data-pagefind-body element... Indexing
         all <body> elements"), which would surface sidebar link text
         and the header row in search results, not just real content. -->
    <article class="flex-1 min-w-0 max-w-[640px] help-article" data-pagefind-body>
      {{ content | safe }}
    </article>
  </div>
</div>
